Live at www.trylumo.co. Case study
updated November 2026.
THE SHORT VERSION
AI made the work of product management cheap: the options, the analysis, the docs, the updates. What's left is the call itself,
and the call is the one part of the job that gets almost no feedback. Outcomes show up months later, mixed in with everything
else, and nobody connects them back to what was believed at the time. AI makes this worse in a way that's easy to miss. It can
write a convincing case for any choice in seconds, so the written reasoning no longer shows what anyone actually thought.
Lumo is a feedback loop for product judgment. You write down your instinct and how sure you are before the model says
anything. Lumo reads the situation back in your own words, names one thing you may have missed, and lays out options without
making the call for you. Then it drafts the update every audience needs. Because the record comes out of work you already
have to do, keeping it takes no extra effort, and over time it shows you where your instincts hold up and where they don't.
I built it end to end and ran a small closed beta with about 15 product managers at early-stage startups. What they valued first
was memory: in chaotic weeks, the record gave them back the context that even AI search tools missed. Next, I want Lumo to
run as a layer inside the AI tools PMs already use, not just as one more app to open.
