01
Why this needs to exist
At my day job, I watched one decision, delaying a release past an election code freeze, get explained four different ways
to four audiences. Two months later, nobody could say why we'd made it. The reasoning had disappeared, and with it any
way to learn whether the call had been right. That's the problem in one story. The rest of this section is why it's getting
bigger, and why now.
THE WORK GOT CHEAP. THE JUDGMENT DIDN'T.
AI now produces the options, the analysis, the spec, and the stakeholder update in minutes. That moves the value of a
product manager toward the one thing a model can't produce for you: the call, and owning it.
AI gave you more options.
It did not give you more judgment.
PRODUCT JUDGMENT GETS ALMOST NO FEEDBACK
Daniel Kahneman and Gary Klein come from opposing schools of thought on intuition, and they still agree on when you
can trust it: when the situation has some structure and the person gets feedback on their judgments. Klein is blunt about
business: "Most corporate decisions aren't going to meet the test of high validity." Product calls are a clear case. The
outcome shows up months later, tangled up with other causes, and nobody links it back to what was believed at the time.
So years of experience don't automatically turn into better judgment. Most of us make every hard call from first principles,
with no record of how our judgment has held up, and we fill the gap with a story about ourselves: I'm a good decision
maker. Kahneman's warning is that people "will feel confident regardless of how well grounded it is in reality." That's a
shaky thing to build long-term strategy on.
AI MAKES THIS WORSE IN TWO WAYS THAT ARE EASY TO MISS
It makes it easier to stop thinking.
In a 2025 Microsoft Research survey of 319 knowledge
workers, higher confidence in generative AI was associated
with less critical thinking. I saw the same thing in my own first
version of Lumo. When it recommended a decision, I
stopped reasoning and started reacting.
It makes every rationale look good.
Kahneman traces overconfidence to how coherent a story
feels, not whether it's true. A model will write a persuasive
case for whichever option you already lean toward, in
seconds. Once that's possible, a written rationale stops being
evidence of what anyone actually thought.
WHY NOW
Producing the work keeps getting cheaper, so judgment is what sets a product manager apart. The same tools make it
easier to hand off the thinking. And as more of the work is produced by AI, the record of what you decided, and why,
becomes the one thing that's unmistakably yours. That makes judgment easier to show and harder to fake. Meanwhile,
models are now reliable enough at structured output that one person could build a tool like this end to end. I know,
because I did.
The point of Lumo
Lumo is a feedback loop for product judgment. It isn't about writing faster, and it isn't another tool that hands you an answer. It
records what you believed before the model said anything, keeps the decision in your hands, and holds onto the record so it can be
checked against what happened. The goal is for experience to turn into better judgment instead of just more confidence.
Sources: Daniel Kahneman and Gary Klein, "Strategic decisions: When can you trust your gut?", McKinsey Quarterly, March 2010. Hao-Ping Lee et al., "The
Impact of Generative AI on Critical Thinking," CHI 2025.
Lumo  |  Case study  |  Terrance Range2 / 12

02
Why today's tools don't fix it
The tools PMs already use split into two groups. Some help in the moment but never see what you believed before they
answered. Others keep the history but make you do all the work.
TOOLGOOD ATWHERE IT FALLS SHORT
Chat assistantThinking through one decision
quickly
Even with memory turned on, it never captures your instinct
before it answers, and it never connects the outcome back to
the call.
Decision journalKeeping an honest historyYou do all the work by hand, so it's the first habit to go in a busy
week.
Docs and SlackSharing with the teamThe reasoning gets lost in threads, and nobody links the
outcome back to the call.
Frameworks like RICEScoring options consistentlyThey score the options. They say nothing about the judgment of
the person choosing.
The insight that makes the record stick
Decision journals fail because they're homework. So in Lumo, the record isn't a separate chore. It comes out of work a PM already
has to do: getting to a decision and telling people about it. The update is why you open Lumo. The record is what you get without
extra effort.
