03
What the beta showed
I ran a small closed beta with about 15 product managers at early-stage startups in health, sports, and edtech. They used
Lumo on real decisions from their own work, and I watched for three things.
•Did people change their first instinct after seeing the options, or did they just confirm what they already believed?
•Did the confidence rating make people stop and think, or did they treat it as a box to tick?
•Where did the model offer options that sounded reasonable but turned out shallow or framed wrong?
The feedback centered on memory. In chaotic stretches, the record helped people remember what had happened and get
the context back. Their point was that even with all the retrieval tools AI offers now, details still get missed, and a
reflection record filled that gap.
On the model itself, the same pattern kept showing up. It's good at widening the set of options and writing clear
messages. It's less reliable at spotting the downside nobody has mentioned, or the political constraint a person already
feels but hasn't put into words. When Lumo helped most, it was usually because it made someone write the trade-off
down, not because it had a better idea. That finding is why I added the "I noticed" line and the "What's making this hard"
field.
One record, three timelines
This week, the update is what gets a PM to open Lumo. In the weeks after, the record is a memory of what happened and why,
which is what the beta valued. Over months, the same record is what makes calibration possible. The first two are working today.
The third is the bet.
This was a small, informal beta, not a controlled study. I read it as direction, not proof.
Lumo  |  Case study  |  Terrance Range4 / 12

04
Who it's for, and why I built it
It's for the working product manager who keeps facing calls with real trade-offs: what to build first, which date to promise,
what to cut. They're short on time, they answer to leadership, and they end up rewriting the same decision for every group
that needs to hear about it.
I'm one of them. My day job is product manager for fundraising at a national digital advocacy platform with 8M+ members,
where a lot of calls have to fit around release freezes during elections. Before product, my doctoral coursework was in
behavioral science and organizational change, the field that studies how people make decisions and how organizations
act on them. And I've founded three companies.
A decision Lumo is built for (illustrative example)
Your largest customer wants single sign-on (SSO) by the end of Q3. Engineering says Q4 at the earliest. Sales has already hinted
yes. Your VP wants an answer by Friday, and whatever you decide, several groups will need to hear it differently. I made this
scenario up to show how Lumo works. It isn't a recorded session, and I use it throughout this document.
