07
How it got here
I started building in July 2026. Most of what makes Lumo work today exists because something I believed turned out to
be wrong once real decisions went through it. Here are the beliefs that changed the product, what actually happened, and
what I did about it.
WHAT I BELIEVEDWHAT HAPPENEDWHAT I CHANGED
The most useful feature
would be a
recommendation.
Within a couple of weeks I hated it. As soon
as the model told me what it would do, I
stopped thinking and started reacting.
I took the recommendation out completely. The
model helps structure the decision, and the PM
makes it.
Good options from the
model would be enough.
In testing, the model was good at widening
the options. It was weak at naming the
political constraint a person already feels
but hasn't said out loud.
I added an optional "What's making this hard" field
and an "I noticed" line in step 2. The field shapes that
line and the tone of the drafts, but Lumo never
quotes it back.
A schema would keep the
model true to what people
wrote.
When the model played the situation back,
it invented or paraphrased the user's words.
Now every part of the framing points to a direct quote
from the user's own text, behind "Show source." The
prompt forbids made-up or paraphrased quotes.
A schema would
guarantee usable output.
Schemas cut down on bad output, but they
don't eliminate it. Broken screens still
slipped through now and then.
I added a second, separate shape check on the
server and one retry. If both attempts fail, the user
gets a plain error and can try again.
Saving decisions would be
enough to learn from
them.
You can't find patterns in decisions you
can't compare.
Every decision now gets a type: timing, scope, hiring,
people, vendor, or strategy. That gives pattern
detection something to group by.
If it worked in preview, it
would work live.
Three bugs showed up only on the live site
(appendix C).
I test the deployed product now, not the preview, and
the app shows whether the AI is up.
The most important decision I made was taking a capability away.
The trade-off I accepted
Taking out the recommendation made Lumo less impressive in a quick demo. A tool that hands you an answer looks smarter in the
first thirty seconds. I chose the version that's more useful on your fifth decision over the one that's more impressive on your first.
Lumo  |  Case study  |  Terrance Range7 / 12

08
Why I built it in this order
If outcome data is what calibration depends on, why ship audience drafts first and outcome check-ins later? Because
nobody records outcomes in a tool they don't open. The update is the reason a PM opens Lumo this week. The record
builds up as a side effect, and the beta showed it's useful as memory long before it's useful for calibration. Outcome
check-ins only pay off once people are coming back, so they come next.
09
Where it's going
Today Lumo is an app you open. Where I want to take it is a decision layer that runs inside the tools product managers
already use. Through plugins and connectors to a PM's AI stack, Lumo could capture the instinct and the call wherever
the decision happens, in a chat, a doc, or a ticket, and pull the outcome back from the same tools once it lands. That's the
version that solves calibration for real, because neither the record nor the outcome depends on someone remembering to
write it down.
It's also why the record matters as a business. It gets more useful the longer you keep it, so the value grows with every
decision instead of resetting with every session.
You have been this sure about a date ten times. Seven of them slipped.
That line comes from the demo on trylumo.co, which runs on a labeled sample record of 40 decisions for a made-up product manager. It
shows the direction, not results. And it's the point of the product. Not a better answer in the moment, but a clearer picture of your own
judgment, built up over real decisions.
#NEXT STEPWHAT IT ADDS
1Outcome check-insAt a set date, ask how the decision actually turned out: as expected, better, or worse,
plus a one-line lesson. Calibration depends on this data.
2Learning from your own historyGroup your past decisions by type, show where your instinct runs better or worse than
you expected, let you set a rule, and bring that rule up the next time a similar call
comes along.
3Connectors into the PM's AI stackPlugins and connectors that bring in context from the tools PMs already use, including
screenshots, voice notes, video clips, and forwarded messages, and pull outcomes
back automatically.
4Research across that contextShow where your instinct and the evidence disagree. The call still stays with you.
5A headless decision layerCapture, record, and calibration running underneath other tools, so a decision is
recorded the same way wherever it's made.
6Better handling of weak optionsMake it quick to flag and dismiss a shallow suggestion, and learn from what gets
dismissed.
7Accounts, then Slack and emailKeep your record across devices, and send drafts straight to where people already
work.
Lumo  |  Case study  |  Terrance Range8 / 12

10
How I'll know if it works, and what doesn't work yet
The real test is calibration. If Lumo works, the calls you rate 4 or 5 out of 5 should turn out well more often than the ones
you rate 1 or 2. Over time, the gap between how sure you felt and how things actually went should shrink. That's what
better judgment looks like when you can measure it.
There are earlier signs to watch for too. People change their first instinct after seeing the options, instead of just
confirming it. They come back to the record when things get chaotic, which the beta already showed. They return to
record how a decision turned out. The "I noticed" line changes how they frame the problem.
Today Lumo can't measure calibration, because it doesn't yet ask how decisions turned out. That's why outcome
check-ins are first on the roadmap.
WHAT STILL DOES NOT WORK WELL
The model sometimes offers options that look reasonable at first and fall apart when you look closer. It can miss how
organizations really work: the stakeholder who'll block the decision, the promise someone made quietly, the constraint
that only lives in people's heads. The "I noticed" line helps, but it doesn't fix this. And confidence ratings are easy to rush.
Because everything is stored in the browser, your record lives on one device. Pattern detection over the long run is still a
demonstration, not something proven on months of real decisions.
11
What this project shows about how I work
I start from the problem, not the model.
The most important design choice was taking a capability
away. Lumo is defined by what the AI isn't allowed to do.
I change the product when an assumption breaks.
The recommendation, the source quotes, and the "I noticed"
line all came from something that didn't work the first time.
I treat AI output as untrusted input.
Schemas, validation, retries, and honest error messages are
product features to me, not cleanup work.
I'm honest about limits.
I can tell you where Lumo works, where it doesn't yet, and
what it would take to prove the rest.
Lumo is my attempt at the part most AI tools still skip: better judgment, and a memory
of how it turned out.
Terrance Range | www.trylumo.co
