B
Appendix: A closer look at the code
Here are three excerpts: two from the route that drafts audience messages, and one from the rate limiter. I trimmed them
for length but didn't change the logic.
CODE   app/api/decide/route.ts (schema, abridged)
const decideTool: Anthropic.Tool = {
  name: "provide_audience_drafts",
  input_schema: { type: "object", properties: {
    claritySummary: { type: "string" },
    drafts: { type: "array", minItems: 2, maxItems: 8, items: {
      properties: {
        // "vp" is required so leadership is never left out
        audienceId: { type: "string" },
        channel: { type: "string", enum: ["email", "slack", "dm"] },
        subject: { type: "string" }, body: { type: "string" },
        pushback: { type: "array", maxItems: 2 /* objection + reply */ },
      },
      additionalProperties: false,
    } },
  } },
}
WHY IT MATTERS
The model can't answer in free text. Every field the screen shows is declared ahead of time, so an empty or
broken draft is an error the server can catch, not a confusing screen for the user.
CODE   app/api/decide/route.ts (retry loop, abridged)
for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= 2 && !result; attempt++) {
  const response = await anthropic.messages.create({
    model: ANTHROPIC_MODEL,
    tools: [decideTool],
    // Force the structured tool; plain text is not an option
    tool_choice: { type: "tool", name: decideTool.name },
    // ...system prompt, user prompt, max_tokens
  })
  const toolResult = response.content.find(isDecideToolUse)
  if (!toolResult) { record("no tool output"); continue }
  // Schema is a request; this check is the guarantee
  if (!isDecideResult(toolResult.input, "vp")) { record("bad shape"); continue }
  result = toolResult.input
}
if (!result && lastFailure)
  return getDiagnosticsResponse(requestStartedAt, attempts, lastFailure)
WHY IT MATTERS
Schemas cut down on bad output but don't eliminate it. A second, independent check on the server and one
retry turned occasional broken screens into rare errors that say clearly what went wrong.
Lumo  |  Case study  |  Terrance Range11 / 12

CODE   lib/rate-limit.ts (abridged)
decide: new Ratelimit({
  redis,
  limiter: Ratelimit.slidingWindow(20, "1 h"),
  prefix: "lumo:ratelimit:decide",
}),
try {
  const { success, reset } = await limiters[route].limit(ip)
  if (success) return { allowed: true }
  return { allowed: false, retryAfter: secondsUntil(reset) }
} catch (error) {
  // A limiter outage must never take the product down with it
  console.error(`[rate-limit] Redis check failed for "${route}"`, error)
  return { allowed: true }
}
WHY IT MATTERS
A free public AI tool needs a cost ceiling, but the limiter protects the product. It isn't the product. So I made it
fail open. If Redis is down or not set up, the request goes through and the failure gets logged.
C
Appendix: What broke in production
The flow reset itself mid-step.
An unstable React key made the
current step reload on every update,
which wiped out what the user had
typed. I fixed the key, then ran all six
steps again in a real browser.
The AI worked in preview, not
live.
Production needed the model
configured explicitly. I moved that
setting into one place and added a
visible AI status and logging.
The sitemap returned a 404.
Vercel sent the bare domain to www,
and my own code sent it back. I made
www the one official address.
What I took from it
Test what's actually deployed, not the preview. Make failures visible. And treat domains and redirects as product work, because if a
search engine can't read your sitemap, people can't find you.
Lumo  |  Case study  |  Terrance Range12 / 12